A research-backed buying guide to red light therapy for the face and the scalp — what the clinical evidence supports, which devices to buy for each job, whether one device can do both, and how to use them so the money is not wasted.

## 1 Executive Summary

**Judgment: no single device has good evidence for both facial skin and hair regrowth. Buy two.** A flexible red/near-infrared face mask handles the face; a scalp-contact laser cap or helmet handles the hair. For most people the best pair is the **Omnilux Contour Face** (~US $395) plus the **iRestore Essential** (~US $695–799), about US $1,100–1,200 in total.

Why that pair:

- **Omnilux Contour Face.** 132 LEDs at 633 nm and 830 nm, about 30 mW/cm² at the skin, FDA-cleared, flexible silicone that sits flat on the face, 10-minute sessions, two-year warranty. Its wavelength pair is the one the original clinical work used, and the 2026 tested roundups (Yahoo Shopping, Healthline, Fortune, Wareable) converge on it as the top flexible mask; the same hardware in the Omnilux Men is Wirecutter's flexible-mask pick.
- **iRestore Essential.** Full-scalp helmet, 120 emitters mixing lasers and LEDs at 650 nm, FDA-cleared, 25 minutes every other day, and a 12-month money-back guarantee with no restocking fee. It is the cheapest full-coverage cleared helmet with a long guarantee. That matters more than diode count: response to light therapy varies by person, no one can predict yours in advance, and the guarantee is what makes the purchase reversible.

**If it must be one device,** the only format that reaches both face and scalp is a small panel such as the **Mito Red Light MitoMIN 2.0** (~US $249, 660 nm + 850 nm). Expect the facial benefit. Treat the hair benefit as uncertain: hair shadows the scalp, panels are not cleared for hair regrowth, and no published trial tests a panel on pattern hair loss.

**Set expectations before spending.** The evidence (PubMed-indexed meta-analyses and randomized trials, cited in Section 3) shows a modest, measurable gain in hair density versus sham devices, roughly +18 hairs/cm² pooled, and a modest, blinded-rater improvement in fine lines after 8–16 weeks. For hair, minoxidil and finasteride remain first-line; light is an add-on. For skin, sunscreen and retinoids remain better proven. Neither device works in weeks: plan on 12 weeks (face) and 16–26 weeks (hair) before judging, and on indefinite maintenance after that.

