## 2 The Core Problem: Face and Scalp Need Different Hardware

Both uses rely on the same mechanism, photobiomodulation: red and near-infrared light absorbed by mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase raises cellular energy output and downstream repair signaling. The delivery problems differ.

- **Face.** The target is the dermis a few millimetres down. A mask with LEDs touching the skin delivers its rated irradiance with no distance loss, covers the whole face at once, and can afford 630–660 nm red plus 830 nm near-infrared. The evidence base is built on 633/830 nm.
- **Scalp.** The target is the follicle, under a layer of hair that absorbs and scatters red light. Every device with sham-controlled trial evidence either presses the emitters against the scalp (helmet, cap) or parts the hair (comb teeth, band). The evidence base is built on 650–680 nm with about 5 mW per emitter.
- **Panels** illuminate both from a distance, but lose 40–60% of rated irradiance at typical 4–6 inch distances (manufacturer figure, EvenSkyn) and cannot part hair. No published randomized trial tests a panel for pattern hair loss.

So "one device for both" is a compromise on the hair side. Buy a panel as a face device that may help a thin-haired scalp, not as a hair device.

## 3 What the Evidence Supports

All trial and meta-analysis data below were retrieved from PubMed; DOIs link to the papers.

### 3.1 Hair (androgenetic alopecia)

**Meta-analyses**

- Perez et al. 2024, *Dermatologic Surgery*: 38 studies, 3,098 patients. Hair density rose significantly versus placebo (standardized mean difference 1.14 under 20 weeks, 1.44 over 20 weeks), with high heterogeneity between studies. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1097/DSS.0000000000004442)
- Alahmadi et al. 2026, *Skin Appendage Disorders*: 37 studies, 3,298 patients. Low-level light added 18.56 hairs/cm² and 10.62 µm of shaft thickness versus sham; adding it to minoxidil gave +11.75 hairs/cm² over minoxidil alone. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1159/000551411)
- Mawu et al. 2025, *Lasers in Medical Science*: 7 randomized trials. Light plus minoxidil beat minoxidil alone by 6.62 hairs/cm², with higher patient satisfaction. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10103-025-04593-7)
- Alosaimi et al. 2025, *Journal of Dermatological Treatment*: 4 randomized trials. No significant difference between minoxidil plus light and minoxidil alone. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1080/09546634.2024.2447355)
- Zhang et al. 2022, *Dermatologic Surgery*: 36 studies. Both red and infrared light were superior to control for androgenetic alopecia. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1097/DSS.0000000000003472)

**Device trials (randomized, double-blind, sham-controlled)**

- HairMax LaserComb, 655 nm, hair-parting teeth: Leavitt et al. 2009, 110 male completers over 26 weeks, greater terminal-hair density than sham (p < 0.0001). [DOI](https://doi.org/10.2165/00044011-200929050-00001) Jimenez et al. 2014, four trials, 269 men and women randomized: +18 to +26 hairs/cm² at 26 weeks versus +2 to +9 with the sham device. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40257-013-0060-6)
- Helmet with 21 lasers and 30 LEDs, all at 655 nm, 25 minutes every other day (about 67 J/cm² per session): Lanzafame et al. 2013, males, 35–39% more hair growth than sham. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1002/lsm.22173) Lanzafame et al. 2014, females, 37% more than sham. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1002/lsm.22277)

**What to take from this**

- The effect is real, modest, and slow. It appears over 16–26 weeks of continuous use, and stopping loses it.
- Whether light adds to minoxidil is contested: two 2025–2026 meta-analyses say yes (by 7–12 hairs/cm²), one says no. Use it as an add-on, not a substitute.
- Lasers and LEDs at the same 655 nm worked together in the Lanzafame helmet trials. No head-to-head trial shows lasers beat LEDs at equal wavelength and dose. The "laser only" argument (for example, the American Hair Loss Association's) rests on penetration theory, not comparative trials. What is true is that laser-cap makers have more published trials than LED-cap makers, whose evidence is company-run (a 31-subject Intertek study for CurrentBody; a consumer survey for HigherDOSE).
- Light needs live follicles. It thickens thinning hair; it does not repopulate long-bald skin.

### 3.2 Face (photoaging, fine lines)

- Russell et al. 2005: Omnilux clinic system at 633 nm and 830 nm, 31 subjects, nine treatments. Profilometry improved at 12 weeks and 81% reported improved periorbital wrinkles. Doses were clinic-level (126 and 66 J/cm²). [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1080/14764170500370059)
- Wunsch and Matuschka 2014: randomized controlled trial, 136 volunteers, 30 sessions. Blinded photo grading, skin roughness, and ultrasound collagen density all improved versus control; broadband light offered no advantage over red alone. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1089/pho.2013.3616)
- Park et al. 2025: multicenter, double-blind, sham-controlled trial of a home LED mask (630 nm and 850 nm, at most 10 mW/cm²), 60 subjects, 16 weeks. Crow's-feet grade improved in 86% of mask users, a 69.5-point difference from the sham group. [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1097/MD.0000000000041596)

**What to take from this:** home masks at 10–30 mW/cm² can produce blinded, measurable wrinkle improvement over 8–16 weeks. The effect is smaller than what retinoids and sunscreen deliver over years; think "softening," not "erasing." Red and near-infrared evidence for acne, melasma, and pigmentation is weak. Blue 415 nm has its own acne evidence but can darken post-inflammatory marks in darker skin.

### 3.3 What FDA clearance means here

The hair devices and most masks above carry FDA 510(k) clearance: the FDA found the device substantially equivalent in safety and intended use to an earlier cleared device. The first was the HairMax LaserComb in January 2007 (K060305) for male pattern hair loss, extended to women in 2011. Clearance is a safety and equivalence bar. It does not rank devices or prove one outperforms another. The thing to avoid is a device with no clearance and no published irradiance, not a device with fewer diodes.

